# Prospective standard reducer experiment

This is source inspection and a plan. No new reducer candidate, semantic run, profile or benchmark exists in this stage. Production and the previous stage remain frozen; `source-inspection.json` verifies every previous inventory file before and after this read-only inspection. It preserves 35 inspected files, three official Node v24.16.0 documents and thirteen source contracts with complete byte fingerprints and line locations.

The behavioral authority is the actual released `@grafana/data@13.2.3` archive, SHA-256 `954a03a68fa122ebe74c0c318dc801e434c17294c5374ef593cf491f73c81c41`, npm gitHead `8ea0d7e31a25f84c2b5a1c173070d8cde8740924`. Retained runtime modules were independently compared to the authenticated archive, and the actual lodash runtime to its authenticated 4.17.23 archive. The current application module is `9269cf8c32be9bf2c075b67019e82e41272d9495f0321fefc17b2c5695a91371`. No candidate imports original code at runtime; original code is a test-only authority.

The specific source-level work to isolate is invariant numeric/null-mode branching. The original standard helper calculates numeric field type once at lines 433–439, then checks that invariant inside each accepted sample at lines 460–488. Null handling at lines 440–456 checks mode on every nullish sample. The current helper at lines 46–84 has the same structure. The target standard workloads are number fields in ignore-null mode, so both conditions are fixed across a call.

The earlier numeric trial `ad42a9ef924fc726e42ca3ef74097d881c67a2da45e10d91fcc16b4f68c123d3` additionally introduced a primitive type guard, many live scalar accumulators, late generic replay and a new result literal. The count trial `4e0e551585b54eea1290b6aff2b9808aa33d3c22c029fe7fde8dd6638af8c8db` also derived counts and changed dispatch. Its clean standard regressions cannot determine the independent cost of branch removal. The separately retained direct-result-literal trial was already rejected. Repeating either combined change would supply weak causal evidence.

The next proposed candidate changes only the standard helper: when values exist, the field type is number/time and ignore-null mode is truthy, dispatch once into a numeric-ignore loop. Keep the existing result object and its nineteen-property initialization. That loop captures raw first/last, skips null and undefined/holes, increments count before the NaN skip, and performs the current sum/extrema/delta/step mutations in the same arithmetic order. It needs no per-sample numeric-field check, null-mode check, primitive-type guard or replay. The existing generic helper remains for other types/modes. Reducer selection, registry, specialized paths, cache merging, defaults and percentile behavior are unchanged.

This is safe to explore within the established ordinary-field/primitive-array contract because dispatch depends on call invariants, not sample contents. A numeric field may still contain primitive strings or booleans: original `sum += value || 0`, comparisons, subtraction and zero checks must retain JavaScript coercion. Do not assume a declared numeric field contains only numbers. Preserve null-as-zero behavior in the generic branch, NaN count/non-null distinctions, raw first/last before null skipping, signed zero, infinities, numeric sentinels, decreasing/reset delta behavior and all nineteen result properties. The direct helper's missing-values and empty-array behavior must remain distinct from reduceField's empty-input shortcuts.

The wrapper must read ordinary values consistently and avoid changing error classes for missing fields. Accessors, proxies, custom array species, mutable defaults/registry extensions and boxed/object samples remain outside established compatibility. That boundary does not justify changing supported primitive coercions. Returning the same object as `state.calcs` is prohibited: the released reducer at lines 180–200 copies results into its cache, so callers may mutate a returned object without changing stored calculations.

Correctness comes before timing. Reuse the exact full 2,910,751 reducer replay and 14,240 helper checks; the reducer count includes 48,240 cache checks. Retain the same source/archive/dependency authentication and corpus/output fingerprints. Add independently shaped long standard-helper cases, including mixed strings/booleans late in number/time fields, sparse/null/NaN runs, sentinels, tiny/overflow values, decreasing/reset sequences, both ignore and zero truthy flags, non-numeric fields and missing values. Compare outputs, all own result/cache properties, input arrays and exception classes against the actual original. Add caller-mutation tests using the original as the oracle to verify result/cache identity separation, complete/partial cache returns and unrelated cache members. Save failed trials under distinct names; no prior receipt is rebound.

Once those pass, a newly coordinated quiet interval may compare original, frozen baseline and the isolated standard candidate with the same five timing workloads. The previously validated isolated percentile helper is a separate candidate; do not combine it with the standard experiment before each change has independent evidence. Report every standard/shared/percentile median and range, including regressions. Compiler branch elimination, inlining, code size and register pressure are hypotheses; source inspection alone establishes no speed improvement. No integration or universal-superiority conclusion follows from this plan.
